\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/artifacts/quick-fix/streams/012-quick-fix.stdout.log:55:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_27\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"sed -n '60,125p' docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"─────────                          ────────────────                ──────────────────────\\\\nsession_start ──────────────────► resolve thread mapping ───────► threads.resolve / newThread\\\\n                                   validate inference config       createSdk({ inference, mode: \\\\\\\"background\\\\\\\", view })\\\\n                                   surface bad pins loudly\\\\nmessage_end ────────────────────► map message → event batch ────► intakeStream.messageEvents(ref, events)\\\\n                                   (1 PI msg → 1..n LHC events)        └─ background scheduler auto-drains\\\\nturn_end ───────────────────────► turn_end event ───────────────► intakeStream.messageEvents(ref, [turn_end])\\\\n                                                                       └─ boundary advance + turn derivations\\\\ncontext ────────────────────────► pull + map ───────────────────► threadView.pull(ref) → ViewMessage[] → AgentMessage[]\\\\nsession_before_compact ─────────► { cancel: true } (LHC owns context; PI compaction off)\\\\n/lh-compact (command) ──────────► threadView.compact(ref, { profile })\\\\n/lh-status, /lh-health ─────────► threadView.status / inspect.health\\\\nagent tools (registerTool) ─────► inspect.overview / health / view, messages.list/show\\\\nModelCall (injected fn) ◄──────── inference adapter calls it ◄──── derivation handlers\\\\n  └─ ctx.modelRegistry.find(provider, model) → complete() → text\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n**Construction:** one `createSdk` per session, `mode: \\\\\\\"background\\\\\\\"` (the scheduler auto-drains on enqueue pokes — the extension never calls `drain`). Built on `session_start`, torn down on `session_shutdown`. SDK instance + thread ref are the only module state.\\\\n\\\\n**Event mapping (the converter contract):** PI `message_end` fans out to `MessageEventInput`s:\\\\n- `user` message → `user_prompt` (text parts joined; images/fileRefs: open question §6)\\\\n- `assistant` message → ordered fan-out per content part: `assistant_thinking`, `assistant_text`, `tool_call` (×N)\\\\n- `toolResult` message → `tool_result` (content text, `isError`)\\\\n- PI `turn_end` event → `turn_end`\\\\n- idempotency keys from session entry id (the POC's `targetEventKey` scheme, simplified)\\\\n\\\\n**Context serving (the mapping back):** `ViewMessage { role: \\\\\\\"user\\\\\\\"|\\\\\\\"assistant\\\\\\\", content }` → PI `AgentMessage`. Band messages are plain user-role text blocks; tail messages are pre-rendered text (tool calls/results already formatted by LHC's mapping table in `shared/view.ts:60-77`). Two things to verify in the dial-in/recording period: whether serving tool calls as flattened text (vs. native `toolCall` content parts) affects model behavior, and what PI requires of the first/last message roles.\\\\n\\\\n**Inference wiring (already specced as Epic 05's host side):** the ~15-line `ModelCall` — `ctx.modelRegistry.find(provider, model)` → missing ⇒ `{ ok: false, kind: \\\\\\\"auth\\\\\\\"|\\\\\\\"invalid_request\\\\\\\" }` → `complete(model, { messages })` → map errors to `ModelCallFailureKind`. Assignments (the seven `kind → { provider, model, prompt }`) come from the extension's config section; `createSdk` throws on incomplete/unknown assignments, and the extension surfaces that `TypeError` at load as a visible, actionable startup error — exactly the startup-validation requirement from the notes doc.\\\\n\\\\n## 4. Functional scope (what the extension must do)\\\\n\\\\n1. **Thread lifecycle** — PI session ↔ LHC thread mapping (persisted via `appendEntry` in the session file, or a registry sidecar like the POC's `threadId-map.json` — decision needed); create on first event; reattach on resume/reload; handle fork (§6).\\\\n2. **Intake** — message/turn event capture, mapped and batched, ordering preserved, idempotent across replays (PI resume re-fires nothing, but reload/fork paths need the keys).\\\\n3. **Context serving** — the `context` hook returns the LHC view; PI's own compaction disabled/intercepted; materialize-to-session-file kept as manual fallback command only.\\\\n4. **Inference host duties** — `ModelCall` + assignments config + startup validation surfacing.\\\\n5. **Operator commands** — compact (with profiles), status, health, sweep, materialize, attach/detach. Far fewer than the POC's 12; the inspect surfaces do the work.\\\\n6. **Agent self-inspection tools** — `registerTool` wrappers over `inspect.*`/`messages.*` reads, same SDK instance. The CLI-replacement decision.\\\\n7. **Status UX** — actionable-only; everything else queryable via health. Footer/widget for passive state (thread id, tail %, compact recommended) is candidate polish.\\\\n8. **Packaging** — npm package under its own name; `packages` setting; works in TUI and headless modes.\\\\n\\\\n## 5. What LHC still needs (host-side gaps, all small)\\\\n\\\\n- **A \\\\\\\"since\\\\\\\" / incremental read for nothing** — actually no: `pull()` returns the full view each call; PI wants full message arrays anyway. No gap.\\\\n- **`turn_end` with no open turn is contractual no-op** — already specced (Epic 01). Good for PI's pre-first-prompt events.\\\\n- **Thread registry by external key** — `threads.resolve` exists; need to confirm it supports lookup by the extension's mapping key or whether the extension owns the map entirely (POC owned it; probably still right).\\\\n- **Nothing else found.** The SDK surface (`intakeStream`, `threadView`, `inspect`, `messages`, `work`) covers every arrow in the diagram. This is the payoff of the SDK-only consolidation.\\\\n\\\\n## 5a. Observed PI event behavior (headless recon, June 12 2026)\\\\n\\\\nDrove published PI 0.79.2 headlessly (`pi --print --mode json -e log-ext.ts`) against gpt-5.4 with a log-every-hook extension, across chatty / tool-heavy / error / resume scenarios. This answers the behavioral questions static types couldn't, and de-risks most of Epic 1's capture contract before M0. Scratch rig: `scratch-pi-recon/`.\\\\n\\\\n**Event lifecycle (verified order):**\\\\n```\\\\nsession_start{reason} → before_agent_start{prompt,images} → agent_start\\\\n  → turn_start{turnIndex}\\\\n     → message_start/message_end (user [text])\\\\n     → message_start/message_end (assistant [thinking?, text?, toolCall×N])\\\\n     → (per tool) tool_execution_start{toolCallId,toolName,args} → tool_call → tool_result → tool_execution_end\\\\n     → message_start/message_end (toolResult [text]) ×N\\\\n     → turn_end{turnIndex, message, toolResults[]}\\\\n  → turn_start{turnIndex+1} ... (a new turn per agent step until no tool calls)\\\\n  → agent_end{messages[]} → session_shutdown\\\\n```\\\\n\\\\n**Answers to the prior unknowns:**\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/artifacts/quick-fix/streams/012-quick-fix.stdout.log:57:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_28\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"rg -n \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\"logger|log\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\(|output|appendLine|console\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\.warn|ctx\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\.log|ctx\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\.logger|ExtensionContext\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\" docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md packages/pi-lhc/src packages/pi-lhc/test -g '\\\\\\\"'!**/streams/**'\\\\\\\"'\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:26:| I-7 | API mismatch (tech-lead #1) | `appendEntry` is on `pi: ExtensionAPI`, not `ctx: ExtensionContext`. | **Moot for mapping, noted for capture.** The mapping no longer writes a session-file pointer, so the `appendEntry` mismatch in the old `session-mapping` is gone. Where the connector does need registration-time API (hook registration), it takes `pi: ExtensionAPI` at the factory, and per-hook handlers take `ctx`. Interface signatures take `pi` where an `ExtensionAPI` method is needed, `ctx` otherwise. |\\\\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:28:| I-9 | Wording (tech-lead #5) | \\\\\\\"no `ctx.ui` surface\\\\\\\" — `ExtensionContext.ui` always exists. | **Resolved.** Reporting guards on headless mode (`hasUI:false`), not on the absence of `ctx.ui`. Test and prose wording corrected. |\\\\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:123:| ModelCall adapter (LHC) | resolved but no text | `kind: \\\\\\\"empty_output\\\\\\\"` — generated by LHC's adapter, never returned by the host function |\\\\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:361:Classification is the adapter-boundary contract and gets depth because it is where a runtime failure becomes a designed error code. A resolved completion with text returns `{ ok: true, text }`. Auth failure and invalid request are terminal (`\\\\\\\"auth\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\"invalid_request\\\\\\\"`); rate-limit, timeout, and network are retryable (`\\\\\\\"rate_limit\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\"timeout\\\\\\\"`, `\\\\\\\"network\\\\\\\"`); a thrown exception classifies as `\\\\\\\"other\\\\\\\"`. The host function never returns `\\\\\\\"empty_output\\\\\\\"` — that kind is generated by LHC's own adapter when a call resolves with no text — so the function returns text-or-transport-failure only, and the no-text case surfaces as the LHC-side `empty_output`.\\\\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:395:Reporting must not assume a TUI. The report uses PI's UI surface (`ctx.ui.notify` when available) and always records the same structured diagnostic in `SessionState.health`; headless modes emit the diagnostic through the headless-appropriate log/output channel. A validation failure leaves capture running — the two concerns are independent — and the affected lane's derivations fail classified and queryable through health (AC-5.3). Assignments load from config; an override of any kind's provider, model, or prompt takes effect on the next session start with no code change.\\\\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:493:export function createModelCall(ctx: ExtensionContext): ModelCall; // ModelCall from lhc\\\\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:513:  ctx: ExtensionContext,\\\\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:517:  ctx: ExtensionContext,\\\\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:523:export function detectFork(ctx: ExtensionContext): ForkInfo | null;\\\\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md:577:- **Turn derivation** — exact inputs (a PI event sequence) to exact outputs (the ordered LHC events plus the single `turn_end`). The worked example in Flow 2 is the first golden case; others cover an aborted run (turn closes complete-but-aborted at `agent_end` with partial content) and a hard-kill (no `agent_end` → open turn left open, tolerated as a no-op on reattach).\\\\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md:52:- The in-extension truncation projection (`prompt-visible-tool-result-projection`) — LHC's visibility boundary (Epic 03/05) owns this now; the extension just serves `pull()` output.\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts:19:  ExtensionContext,\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts:78:  buildSdkConfig?: (ctx: ExtensionContext) => OpResult<SdkConfig>;\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts:84:    ctx: ExtensionContext,\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts:148:  ctx: ExtensionContext,\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts:193:  ctx: ExtensionContext,\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts:232:    ((ctx: ExtensionContext) => defaultBuildSdkConfig(ctx, deps.assignmentConfig));\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts:244:  function buildResolveDeps(ctx: ExtensionContext): ResolveDeps {\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts:255:    ctx: ExtensionContext,\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts:275:    ctx: ExtensionContext,\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/synthetic.ts:104:    content: [{ type: \\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\", text: opts.content ?? \\\\\\\"tool output\\\\\\\" }],\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.test.ts:11:// depends on these corpora being trustworthy, so the loader's output is checked\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/src/inference/startup-validation.ts:15:import type { ExtensionContext } from \\\\\\\"../pi/types.js\\\\\\\";\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/src/inference/startup-validation.ts:37:  ctx: ExtensionContext,\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/src/inference/startup-validation.ts:88:  ctx: ExtensionContext,\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/src/inference/model-call.ts:16:import type { ExtensionContext, ModelHandle } from \\\\\\\"../pi/types.js\\\\\\\";\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/src/inference/model-call.ts:99:export function createModelCall(ctx: ExtensionContext, deps: ModelCallDeps = {}): ModelCall {\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/src/inference/model-call.ts:133:      // empty_output is classified by LHC's adapter, not by the host function.\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/src/pi/types.ts:97:  ctx: ExtensionContext,\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/src/pi/types.ts:103:export type PiCommandHandler = (args: string[], ctx: ExtensionContext) => void | Promise<void>;\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/src/pi/types.ts:110:  run: (input: unknown, ctx: ExtensionContext) => unknown | Promise<unknown>;\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/src/pi/types.ts:124:// ── Per-hook context (the `ctx` object, ExtensionContext) ────────────\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/src/pi/types.ts:156:export interface ExtensionContext {\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/src/pi/types.ts:206:  outputTokens?: number;\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/inference/model-call.test.ts:6:// TC-4.4: Resolved-but-no-output → empty_output by adapter (not host fn).\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/inference/model-call.test.ts:14:import type { ExtensionContext, ModelHandle } from \\\\\\\"../../src/pi/types.js\\\\\\\";\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/inference/model-call.test.ts:29:      const ctx: ExtensionContext = {\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/inference/model-call.test.ts:68:      const ctx: ExtensionContext = {\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/inference/model-call.test.ts:119:        const ctx: ExtensionContext = {\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/inference/model-call.test.ts:141:        const ctx: ExtensionContext = {\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/inference/model-call.test.ts:235:  describe(\\\\\\\"TC-4.4: Host returns text-or-transport-failure; adapter owns empty_output\\\\\\\", () => {\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/inference/model-call.test.ts:236:    it(\\\\\\\"returns ok:true with empty text when completion produces no output\\\\\\\", async () => {\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/inference/model-call.test.ts:237:      const ctx: ExtensionContext = {\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/inference/model-call.test.ts:260:    it(\\\\\\\"LHC adapter classifies empty resolved output as empty_output\\\\\\\", async () => {\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/inference/model-call.test.ts:288:            idempotencyKey: \\\\\\\"empty-output-prompt\\\\\\\",\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/inference/model-call.test.ts:289:            payload: { text: \\\\\\\"produce no output\\\\\\\" },\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/inference/model-call.test.ts:302:              reason: expect.stringContaining(\\\\\\\"empty_output\\\\\\\"),\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/inference/model-call.test.ts:303:              lastError: expect.stringContaining(\\\\\\\"empty_output\\\\\\\"),\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/inference/startup-validation.test.ts:11:import type { ExtensionContext, ModelHandle } from \\\\\\\"../../src/pi/types.js\\\\\\\";\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/inference/startup-validation.test.ts:16:/** Create a minimal ExtensionContext for testing. */\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/inference/startup-validation.test.ts:21:): ExtensionContext {\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/inference/startup-validation.test.ts:282:        const ctx: ExtensionContext = {\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/inference/closed-loop.test.ts:13:import type { ExtensionContext } from \\\\\\\"../../src/pi/types.js\\\\\\\";\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/inference/closed-loop.test.ts:43:  ctx: ExtensionContext,\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/inference/closed-loop.test.ts:63:  let ctx: ExtensionContext;\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/src/lifecycle/fork.ts:6:import type { ExtensionContext, SessionStartEvent } from \\\\\\\"../pi/types.js\\\\\\\";\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/src/lifecycle/fork.ts:23:  ctx: ExtensionContext,\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/smoke/extension-load.test.ts:10:import type { ExtensionAPI, ExtensionContext, PiHookName } from \\\\\\\"../../src/pi/types.js\\\\\\\";\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/smoke/extension-load.test.ts:49:function syntheticCtx(marker: string): ExtensionContext {\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/lifecycle/thread-resolution.test.ts:24:import type { ExtensionContext } from \\\\\\\"../../src/pi/types.js\\\\\\\";\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/lifecycle/thread-resolution.test.ts:69:function syntheticCtx(cwd: string): ExtensionContext {\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/lifecycle/thread-resolution.test.ts:263:    const ctx: ExtensionContext = {\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/capture/support.ts:22:import type { ExtensionContext, SessionStartReason } from \\\\\\\"../../src/pi/types.js\\\\\\\";\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/capture/support.ts:29:export function syntheticCtx(cwd = \\\\\\\"/work/capture\\\\\\\"): ExtensionContext {\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/capture/support.ts:65:  ctx: ExtensionContext;\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/lifecycle/instance.test.ts:21:import type { ExtensionContext } from \\\\\\\"../../src/pi/types.js\\\\\\\";\\\\npackages/pi-lhc/test/lifecycle/instance.test.ts:43:function syntheticCtx(cwd: string): ExtensionContext {\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/artifacts/epic/streams/003-epic-review.stdout.log:5715:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_52\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md | sed -n '35,80p;120,150p;228,236p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"    35\\t### Flow Summary\\n    36\\t\\n    37\\t| Flow | Capability | Surface (tech arch) |\\n    38\\t|------|------------|---------------------|\\n    39\\t| 1 | Session lifecycle and launch-driven thread resolution | Session Lifecycle |\\n    40\\t| 2 | Event capture and turn derivation | Event Capture |\\n    41\\t| 3 | Fork as new thread | Session Lifecycle + Event Capture |\\n    42\\t| 4 | Inference host routing | Inference Host |\\n    43\\t| 5 | Startup validation and assignment config | Inference Host |\\n    44\\t| 6 | Capture verification | Event Capture (read-back via inspect) |\\n    45\\t\\n    46\\t---\\n    47\\t\\n    48\\t## User Profile\\n    49\\t\\n    50\\t**Primary User:** A developer using PI as their daily coding agent, with the `pi-lhc` extension loaded.\\n    51\\t**Context:** Running normal coding sessions — chatty exchanges, tool-heavy agent runs, sessions resumed across days, the occasional fork or mid-task kill. They are not interacting with LHC directly; capture runs in the background.\\n    52\\t**Mental Model:** \\\"I use PI exactly as before. In the background my whole session is recorded into a durable thread, so my context can be managed later. Nothing about how the agent behaves changes yet.\\\"\\n    53\\t**Key Constraint:** This epic is observe-only. The extension must add no perceptible latency and must change nothing the model sees — PI's native context handling stays in control. A capture failure must never break the PI session: writable-thread failures become durable, queryable gaps; store-unavailable failures become visible extension diagnostics because the gap cannot be written.\\n    54\\t\\n    55\\t---\\n    56\\t\\n    57\\t## Scope\\n    58\\t\\n    59\\t### In Scope\\n    60\\t\\n    61\\t- LHC instance lifecycle: initialize on session start, dispose on shutdown, reconstruct on reload — holding only plain data (thread reference, told-the-user flags) across hooks.\\n    62\\t- Launch-driven thread resolution: a new thread by default, a cwd-scoped picker (`--resume`/`-r`), the most recent thread (`--continue`/`-c`), or a named thread (`--session <id>`, partial-id match) — all resolved through the registry catalog.\\n    63\\t- Event capture: PI messages and turns mapped to LHC intake events (the productionized converter), preserving order and idempotency.\\n    64\\t- Runtime-change capture: model-select and thinking-level-select changes captured in order as `runtime_note` events, because they are observable only in-stream.\\n    65\\t- Turn derivation: LHC turn boundaries derived from PI traffic, not taken one-to-one from PI's per-agent-step `turn_end`.\\n    66\\t- Fork handling: a new thread per fork, seeded by replaying the source thread to the fork point, source unchanged.\\n    67\\t- The injected model-call function over PI's model registry and auth.\\n    68\\t- Model-assignment config (the seven derivation kinds) and startup validation with visible, actionable reporting.\\n    69\\t- Capture verification: recorded corpora replay identically through the converter; inspect surfaces reflect the captured session.\\n    70\\t\\n    71\\t### Out of Scope\\n    72\\t\\n    73\\t- Serving context to the model — Feature 2 / Epic 2. PI's native context handling runs as-is throughout this epic.\\n    74\\t- Intercepting or disabling PI's own compaction — Feature 2 / Epic 2.\\n    75\\t- Operator commands and agent self-inspection tools beyond what capture verification reads — Feature 3 / Epic 3.\\n    76\\t- Status-bar / footer UX beyond actionable startup-validation reporting — Feature 3 / Epic 3.\\n    77\\t- Packaging, the runner CLI, headless-mode conformance as a deliverable — Feature 4 / Epic 4.\\n    78\\t- Web-search, service-tier, and researcher-subagent extensions — Feature 5 / Epic 5.\\n    79\\t- Derivation prompt and model dial-in (quality tuning) — the paired M0 dial-in period; this epic ships default assignments and proves the routing, not the tuning.\\n    80\\t\\n   120\\t  else --session id\\n   121\\t    E->>R: resolve(id, partial match)\\n   122\\t  end\\n   123\\t  E->>L: initLhc (background) on resolved thread\\n   124\\t  Note over E,L: hooks fire — extension holds only threadRef and flags\\n   125\\t  OP->>E: session_shutdown (reason)\\n   126\\t  E->>L: dispose (flush/cleanup)\\n   127\\t```\\n   128\\t\\n   129\\t**AC-1.1:** On `session_start` with no thread-selecting launch flag, the extension creates a new LHC thread (registering it in the catalog with its cwd), initializes one LHC instance in background scheduler mode against it, and does not drive the derivation queue itself.\\n   130\\t\\n   131\\t**AC-1.2:** On `session_start` that resolves to an existing thread (via `--continue`, `--session`, or a picker selection), the extension initializes against that thread by resolving it through the registry. No second thread is created for an already-resolved thread.\\n   132\\t\\n   133\\t**AC-1.3:** Across all hooks, the extension holds only plain data (thread reference, file path, told-the-user flags) between events. It retains no PI context or session-manager object across hook boundaries; each handler uses the fresh context PI provides. A session replacement (new/resume/fork) that invalidates prior context objects does not break capture.\\n   134\\t\\n   135\\t**AC-1.4:** On `session_shutdown`, the extension disposes the LHC instance with flush and cleanup. A subsequent run that resolves the same thread finds it complete through the last event recorded before shutdown — no trailing loss.\\n   136\\t\\n   137\\t**AC-1.5:** On reload (extension torn down and re-initialized while the same session continues), the extension reconstructs the thread reference from durable state (the resolved thread id), not from retained in-memory objects, and capture continues on the same thread.\\n   138\\t\\n   139\\t**AC-1.6:** Launch resolves the thread by mode: no flag creates a new thread; `--session <id>` resolves a named thread by full or partial id; `--continue`/`-c` resolves the most recently created thread; an ambiguous or unresolvable id fails with an actionable message rather than silently creating a new thread.\\n   140\\t\\n   141\\t**AC-1.7:** `--resume`/`-r` lists threads scoped to the current working directory, each shown with its title and creation time, and resolves the operator's selection. With no threads for the cwd, the picker reports an empty list rather than failing.\\n   142\\t\\n   143\\t---\\n   144\\t\\n   145\\t## Flow 2: Event Capture and Turn Derivation\\n   146\\t\\n   147\\tFrom the first prompt on, the extension maps each finalized PI message to LHC intake events and records them in order. PI fires a turn boundary per agent step; an LHC turn spans a whole exchange (one user prompt and everything the agent did in response). The converter derives LHC turn boundaries from PI traffic rather than copying PI's. Capture is duplicate-safe; writable-thread failures become durable gaps, store-unavailable failures become visible extension diagnostics, and neither breaks the session.\\n   148\\t\\n   149\\t```mermaid\\n   150\\tsequenceDiagram\\n   228\\t\\n   229\\tCapture is verified without serving anything to a model. A recorded PI corpus replays through the converter; the resulting thread's read-back is matched against the fixture's expectation. Replay is deterministic: the same corpus yields the same thread every time. Inspect surfaces report the captured session — event and message counts, last recorded position, and any gaps.\\n   230\\t\\n   231\\t**AC-6.1:** Replaying a recorded PI corpus through the converter produces a thread whose read-back — events, messages, and turns — matches the fixture's expectation, including the chatty, tool-heavy, parallel-tool, error-result, and aborted-turn corpora.\\n   232\\t\\n   233\\t**AC-6.2:** Replay is deterministic: the same corpus replayed twice produces identical thread read-back. The deterministic-ID property holds through the converter, so a re-replay does not perturb IDs or ordering.\\n   234\\t\\n   235\\t**AC-6.3:** inspect surfaces (overview, health) reflect the captured session: event and message counts, the last recorded event position, and any capture gaps or failed derivations are visible rather than hidden.\\n   236\\t\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/artifacts/epic/streams/003-epic-review.stdout.log:5716:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_53\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md | sed -n '1,36p;185,225p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"     1\\t# Epic 1: Connector Core — Tech Design\\n     2\\t\\n     3\\t**Status:** Draft for review\\n     4\\t**Epic:** `epic.md`\\n     5\\t**PRD:** `../00-prd.md` — Feature 1\\n     6\\t**Tech Arch:** `../01-tech-arch.md`\\n     7\\t**Wiring research:** `../notes/pi-ext-integration-research.md` (verified against PI v0.79.2)\\n     8\\t**Test plan:** `test-plan.md`\\n     9\\t\\n    10\\t---\\n    11\\t\\n    12\\t## Spec Validation\\n    13\\t\\n    14\\tThe epic is implementable as written once its named M0 inputs land. Every AC maps to a module and a test; the data contracts match the real surfaces the design calls into (the LHC SDK at `packages/lhc`, the PI extension API at `@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent` v0.79.2, the completion API at `@earendil-works/pi-ai`). The tech-design questions are answered below, and the places where the design takes a position the epic left open are recorded as deviations with rationale.\\n    15\\t\\n    16\\tThe validation that mattered most: the epic's load-bearing claim is that an LHC turn is not a PI turn, and the converter must derive LHC turn boundaries from the agent-run bracket rather than from PI's per-step `turn_end`. The real PI event types confirm the shape the epic relies on — `turn_end` carries a per-agent-run `turnIndex` that resets to 0 each run, and `agent_end` brackets the whole run — so the contract is buildable against types that exist, not against an assumption.\\n    17\\t\\n    18\\t### Issues Found\\n    19\\t\\n    20\\t| # | Type | Issue | Resolution |\\n    21\\t|---|------|-------|------------|\\n    22\\t| I-1 | TDQ-1 (dissolved) | Mapping persistence shape (session file vs sidecar vs both). | **Dissolved — the thread is resolved at launch, not from a stored mapping.** The LHC thread is the system of record and the registry is the catalog; the thread for a run is named by the launch (new / `--session` / `--continue` / `--resume` picker), so there is no PI-session→thread mapping to persist, no early-crash window, and no recovery sidecar. The tech-lead's durability finding is moot: there is no pointer to lose. (PI's own session file still exists in this observe-only epic — PI runs its session normally; it just is not where LHC's thread identity lives.) See Flow 1. |\\n    23\\t| I-2 | TDQ-2 | Fork derived-form reuse — provable-safe copy vs always-requeue. | **Resolved for v1: always requeue.** The new thread is seeded by event replay only; derived forms are not copied across the fork. Reuse is a later optimization gated on a provenance-identity check that does not exist yet. The epic's AC-3.3 permits this (reuse \\\"may\\\" happen; requeue is the correct fallback). Recorded as a deviation — the epic leaves the door open, the design closes it for v1. See Deferred Items. |\\n    24\\t| I-3 | TDQ-3 | `thinkingSignature` / `thoughtSignature` handling. | **Resolved: capture, do not interpret.** Assistant thinking carries an opaque `thinkingSignature`; tool calls carry an opaque `thoughtSignature` (the POC mapper found both). Neither is part of the seven LHC intake payloads, which are text/structured-arg only. The converter records thinking text and tool-call arguments; the signatures are dropped in this epic because nothing in capture or derivation consumes them. Flagged as a deviation: re-injecting signatures is a serving concern (Epic 2), not capture. |\\n    25\\t| I-4 | TDQ-4 (dissolved) | Thread resolution by external key. | **Dissolved.** Resolution is by the launch input (full/partial `threadId`, or a picker selection over `listThreads`), through `threads.resolve` / `threads.resolveThreadRef`. There is no external PI-session key to translate, because the operator names the thread at launch. |\\n    26\\t| I-7 | API mismatch (tech-lead #1) | `appendEntry` is on `pi: ExtensionAPI`, not `ctx: ExtensionContext`. | **Moot for mapping, noted for capture.** The mapping no longer writes a session-file pointer, so the `appendEntry` mismatch in the old `session-mapping` is gone. Where the connector does need registration-time API (hook registration), it takes `pi: ExtensionAPI` at the factory, and per-hook handlers take `ctx`. Interface signatures take `pi` where an `ExtensionAPI` method is needed, `ctx` otherwise. |\\n    27\\t| I-8 | API mismatch (tech-lead #3) | `registryPath` is not a field of `SdkConfig`. | **Resolved.** `registryPath` is removed from `initLhc`/`createSdk` construction. It is a per-operation argument on the `threads` calls (`newThread`, `resolve`, `listThreads`), which is where the real LHC API accepts it. `initLhc` is the rename of `createSdk` and takes `SdkConfig` unchanged. |\\n    28\\t| I-9 | Wording (tech-lead #5) | \\\"no `ctx.ui` surface\\\" — `ExtensionContext.ui` always exists. | **Resolved.** Reporting guards on headless mode (`hasUI:false`), not on the absence of `ctx.ui`. Test and prose wording corrected. |\\n    29\\t| I-10 | Gap fill | Runtime metadata: model/thinking changes, cwd, foreign-extension state. | **Resolved per epic A-8/A-9.** Model-select and thinking-level-select are captured as `runtime_note` events (AC-2.8, in-stream-only). cwd is stored on the registry row at thread creation and scopes the `--resume` picker. The full PI-runtime-state restoration inventory is an Epic 2 prerequisite (tech arch). Foreign-extension session persistence is out of scope v1 (epic A-9). |\\n    30\\t| I-5 | TDQ-5 | Converter structure — how it consumes `message_end` and emits batched `MessageEventInput[]`. | **Resolved.** The converter is two pure stages plus one stateful accumulator: `mapMessage` (one PI `AgentMessage` → ordered `MessageEventInput[]`, no I/O) and `deriveTurnClose` (decides when to emit `turn_end`), driven by a per-session `TurnAccumulator` that tracks the open LHC turn across `message_end`/`agent_end`. Batches flush through `intakeStream.messageEvents(threadRef, events)`. See Flow 2 design. |\\n    31\\t| I-6 | Deviation | Epic A-7 names image/file-reference handling an M0 decision. | **Design position pending M0, with a safe fallback specified.** The current LHC intake schema has no image/file payload kind beyond text/runtime-note material. Until M0 chooses placeholder vs. schema extension, unsupported content parts degrade to a `runtime_note` event recording the omission (never silent drop). If M0 extends the intake schema, the converter gains kinds; the degrade-to-`runtime_note` path remains the fallback for unsupported parts. This unblocks implementation planning without pre-empting the M0 call. |\\n    32\\t\\n    33\\t---\\n    34\\t\\n    35\\t## Context\\n    36\\t\\n   185\\t| `lifecycle/picker.ts` | NEW | `--resume` cwd-scoped thread list (title + created) and selection | `lhc` threads (`listThreads`) | AC-1.7 |\\n   186\\t| `lifecycle/state.ts` | NEW | Plain-data-only holder and extension diagnostics; never caches a PI context/session object across hooks | — | AC-1.3, AC-2.7 |\\n   187\\t| `lifecycle/fork.ts` | NEW | Detect fork from `session_before_fork` hook (PI session tree as Epic-1 fallback); new thread; replay-seed to fork point; never write source | `lhc` threads, capture | AC-3.1, AC-3.2, AC-3.3 |\\n   188\\t| `capture/converter.ts` | NEW | Orchestrate map→accumulate→batch; isolate capture failure to a gap/health signal | map-message, turn-accumulator, `lhc` intakeStream | AC-2.1, AC-2.7 |\\n   189\\t| `capture/map-message.ts` | NEW | Pure map of one `AgentMessage` to ordered events; fan-out; parallel-tool correlation; error-result; abort; unsupported→runtime_note | idempotency | AC-2.1, AC-2.3, AC-2.4, AC-2.5 |\\n   190\\t| `capture/turn-accumulator.ts` | NEW | Track the open LHC turn; emit exactly one `turn_end` at `agent_end`; ignore per-step PI `turn_end` | — | AC-2.2 |\\n   191\\t| `capture/runtime-changes.ts` | NEW | Map `model_select` / `thinking_level_select` hooks to ordered `runtime_note` events (new + previous value) | `lhc` intakeStream | AC-2.8 |\\n   192\\t| `capture/idempotency.ts` | NEW | Construct a stable key per event so re-delivery dedups | — | AC-2.6 |\\n   193\\t| `inference/model-call.ts` | NEW | Resolve (provider,model) via registry; complete; classify failures; multi-lane | PI modelRegistry, pi-ai | AC-4.1, AC-4.2, AC-4.3, AC-4.4 |\\n   194\\t| `inference/assignments.ts` | NEW | Load seven assignments from config; fail loud on missing/unknown/placeholder; override on next start | — | AC-5.4, AC-5.5 |\\n   195\\t| `inference/startup-validation.ts` | NEW | Probe each assignment with `modelRegistry.find(provider, model)` for existence and `hasConfiguredAuth(model)` / `getAvailable()` membership for configured auth; report unknown and not-logged-in lanes through `ctx.ui`/headless diagnostics; leave capture running | PI modelRegistry | AC-5.1, AC-5.2, AC-5.3 |\\n   196\\t| `verify/replay.ts` | NEW | Replay a corpus through the converter; compare read-back; deterministic | converter, `lhc` inspect | AC-6.1, AC-6.2 |\\n   197\\t| (closed-loop, in `model-call` + scheduler) | NEW | Captured thread's queued derivation invokes the function and records an outcome | `lhc` scheduler, inspect | AC-4.5 |\\n   198\\t| `inspect` reads (via `lhc`) | EXISTS | Surface counts, position, gaps, failures | `lhc` inspect | AC-6.3 |\\n   199\\t\\n   200\\t### Component Interaction\\n   201\\t\\n   202\\t```mermaid\\n   203\\tsequenceDiagram\\n   204\\t  participant PI\\n   205\\t  participant IDX as index.ts\\n   206\\t  participant LC as lifecycle\\n   207\\t  participant CV as converter\\n   208\\t  participant IS as lhc.intakeStream\\n   209\\t  participant SCH as lhc scheduler\\n   210\\t  participant MC as model-call\\n   211\\t  PI->>IDX: session_start\\n   212\\t  IDX->>LC: resolve/create thread, initLhc(background)\\n   213\\t  PI->>IDX: message_end / turn_end / agent_end\\n   214\\t  IDX->>CV: map + accumulate\\n   215\\t  CV->>IS: messageEvents(threadRef, events)\\n   216\\t  Note over SCH: background scheduler drains on its own\\n   217\\t  SCH->>MC: ModelCall(provider, model, messages)\\n   218\\t  MC->>PI: modelRegistry.find + complete\\n   219\\t  PI->>IDX: session_shutdown\\n   220\\t  IDX->>LC: dispose(flush)\\n   221\\t```\\n   222\\t\\n   223\\t---\\n   224\\t\\n   225\\t## Flow-by-Flow Design\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/artifacts/epic/streams/003-epic-review.stdout.log:5718:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_54\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba packages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts | sed -n '134,160p;300,308p;466,470p;675,680p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"   134\\tconst THREAD_STATE_ENTRY_TYPE = \\\"pi-lhc-thread-state\\\";\\n   135\\tinterface DurableThreadStateEntry {\\n   136\\t  version: 1;\\n   137\\t  threadRef: ThreadRef;\\n   138\\t}\\n   139\\t\\n   140\\tfunction durableThreadStateFromEntries(\\n   141\\t  entries: readonly { readonly [k: string]: unknown }[],\\n   142\\t): DurableThreadStateEntry | null {\\n   143\\t  for (let index = entries.length - 1; index >= 0; index -= 1) {\\n   144\\t    const entry = entries[index];\\n   145\\t    if (entry === undefined) continue;\\n   146\\t    if (entry.type !== \\\"custom\\\" || entry.customType !== THREAD_STATE_ENTRY_TYPE) continue;\\n   147\\t    const data = entry.data;\\n   148\\t    if (typeof data !== \\\"object\\\" || data === null) continue;\\n   149\\t    const candidate = data as { readonly [k: string]: unknown };\\n   150\\t    if (candidate.version !== 1) continue;\\n   151\\t    const threadRef = candidate.threadRef;\\n   152\\t    if (typeof threadRef !== \\\"object\\\" || threadRef === null) continue;\\n   153\\t    const ref = threadRef as { readonly [k: string]: unknown };\\n   154\\t    if (typeof ref.threadId === \\\"string\\\" || typeof ref.filePath === \\\"string\\\") {\\n   155\\t      return { version: 1, threadRef: ref as unknown as ThreadRef };\\n   156\\t    }\\n   157\\t  }\\n   158\\t  return null;\\n   159\\t}\\n   160\\t\\n   300\\t  async function resolveReloadFor(\\n   301\\t    launch: LaunchFlags,\\n   302\\t    ctx: ExtensionContext,\\n   303\\t  ): Promise<OpResult<ThreadRef | null>> {\\n   304\\t    const durable = durableThreadStateFromEntries(ctx.sessionManager.getEntries());\\n   305\\t    if (durable !== null) return { ok: true, value: durable.threadRef };\\n   306\\t    return resolveReloadThread(launch, buildResolveDeps(ctx));\\n   307\\t  }\\n   308\\t\\n   466\\t    }\\n   467\\t\\n   468\\t    state = createSessionState(resolved.value);\\n   469\\t    appendThreadState?.({ version: 1, threadRef: state.threadRef });\\n   470\\t    const piSessionId = sessionIdOf(resolved.value);\\n   675\\t  return {\\n   676\\t    handlers,\\n   677\\t    register(pi: ExtensionAPI): void {\\n   678\\t      appendThreadState = (entry) => pi.appendEntry(THREAD_STATE_ENTRY_TYPE, entry);\\n   679\\t      for (const name of EPIC_1_HOOKS) pi.registerHook(name, handlers[name]);\\n   680\\t    },\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/artifacts/epic/streams/003-epic-review.stdout.log:5720:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_55\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"nl -ba packages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts | sed -n '1,12p;137,149p'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"     1\\t// Recorded-corpus loader (tech design §Fixture Contracts). Each corpus is a\\n     2\\t// named lifecycle paired with the LHC intake events it should produce. Until M0\\n     3\\t// delivers real recordings, the loaders build synthetic stand-ins; the\\n     4\\t// machinery (load → validate shape → compare on replay in Story 3) is real and\\n     5\\t// widens as corpora arrive. The validator PARSES and CHECKS shapes — it never\\n     6\\t// hardcodes a passing count (story §Anti-Shim Requirements).\\n     7\\timport type { EventKind } from \\\"lhc\\\";\\n     8\\timport type { Corpus } from \\\"../../src/verify/replay.js\\\";\\n     9\\timport { ABORTED_DISPOSITION_TEXT } from \\\"../../src/capture/map-message.js\\\";\\n    10\\timport {\\n    11\\t  makeAssistantMessage,\\n    12\\t  makeToolResult,\\n   137\\texport const CORPUS_LOADERS = {\\n   138\\t  chatty: loadChattyCorpus,\\n   139\\t  \\\"tool-heavy\\\": loadToolHeavyCorpus,\\n   140\\t  \\\"parallel-tool\\\": loadParallelToolCorpus,\\n   141\\t  \\\"error-result\\\": loadErrorResultCorpus,\\n   142\\t  \\\"aborted-turn\\\": loadAbortedTurnCorpus,\\n   143\\t} as const;\\n   144\\t\\n   145\\texport type CorpusName = keyof typeof CORPUS_LOADERS;\\n   146\\t\\n   147\\texport function loadAllCorpora(): Corpus[] {\\n   148\\t  return Object.values(CORPUS_LOADERS).map((load) => load());\\n   149\\t}\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/artifacts/epic/streams/003-epic-review.stdout.log:5723:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_57\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"{\\\"outcome\\\":\\\"block\\\",\\\"crossStoryFindings\\\":[\\\"Stories 2, 4, 5, and 6 are mostly coherent: capture, turn derivation, fork replay, inference routing, and startup validation all run through connector entry points and real LHC surfaces.\\\",\\\"Story 1 thread identity drift remains material: the current code both adds a PI custom-entry thread pointer and still keeps a cwd-most-recent fallback, while the epic and tech design describe launch/registry resolution with no PI-session-to-thread mapping.\\\",\\\"Story 3 does not meet the epic-level verification contract because the corpus loaders are synthetic stand-ins rather than recorded PI corpora.\\\"],\\\"architectureFindings\\\":[\\\"Epic 1 correctly remains observe-only: index.ts registers no context hook and capture does not alter what PI serves to the model.\\\",\\\"The converter architecture matches the tech design: map-message is pure, turn-accumulator owns the LHC turn boundary, and converter writes through intakeStream.messageEvents.\\\",\\\"The current reload implementation conflicts with the tech design resolution of I-1/I-7: tech-design.md says there is no stored PI-session-to-thread pointer, but index.ts writes and reads a pi-lhc-thread-state custom entry via appendEntry/sessionManager.\\\",\\\"The production --resume default does not resolve an operator selection. It notifies the candidate list, auto-resolves only one candidate, and returns null for multiple candidates.\\\",\\\"Fork seeding uses the real intake path and source read-back is not written, but the fork path uses a filePath ThreadRef for the target, which is another sign that lifecycle thread identity is not consistently registry-id based.\\\"],\\\"epicCoverageAssessment\\\":[\\\"Flow 1 is incomplete against the written architecture and AC-1.7: launch resolution exists, but reload identity and resume selection still require human ruling or design update.\\\",\\\"Flow 2 is covered by source and tests: ordered message mapping, turn derivation, runtime notes, idempotency, gaps, parallel tools, errors, and aborts are implemented.\\\",\\\"Flow 3 is implemented with replay seeding and derived-form requeue; source immutability is covered.\\\",\\\"Flow 4 is implemented through createModelCall over PI modelRegistry plus pi-ai dynamic import, with classified failures and closed-loop persisted outcomes covered.\\\",\\\"Flow 5 is implemented: assignments load defaults/overrides, startup validation reports unreachable lanes, and capture continues.\\\",\\\"Flow 6 is not fully covered: replay is deterministic and compares real read-back, but it consumes synthetic fixtures instead of recorded PI corpora required by AC-6.1.\\\"],\\\"productionPathFindings\\\":[\\\"No fake adapter was found in src capture/fork/inspect paths: capture uses real LHC intakeStream, fork replay uses intakeStream.messageEvents, and inspect reads real LHC surfaces.\\\",\\\"Test-only model fakes are confined to test/fixtures and are not production code.\\\",\\\"createModelCall does not fabricate output when pi-ai is missing; it returns a classified failure.\\\",\\\"Material non-real verification path: packages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts explicitly says loaders build synthetic stand-ins until M0 delivers recordings, so Flow 6 is not proving recorded PI corpus replay.\\\",\\\"Material architecture drift: production index.ts persists threadRef in PI custom entries, despite tech-design.md stating the mapping persistence problem is dissolved and no session-file pointer is written.\\\",\\\"Material resume limitation: production defaultSelectThread has no interactive operator selection path for multiple cwd-scoped threads.\\\"],\\\"blockingFindings\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"EV-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"major\\\",\\\"title\\\":\\\"Capture verification uses synthetic stand-ins instead of recorded PI corpora\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"epic.md:69 and epic.md:229-231 require recorded corpora replay through the converter. packages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts:1-5 states the loaders build synthetic stand-ins until M0 delivers real recordings; loadAllCorpora at lines 137-149 returns only those synthetic loaders.\\\",\\\"affectedFiles\\\":[\\\"docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/epic.md\\\",\\\"packages/pi-lhc/test/fixtures/corpus.ts\\\",\\\"packages/pi-lhc/src/verify/replay.ts\\\",\\\"packages/pi-lhc/test/verify/replay.test.ts\\\"],\\\"requirementIds\\\":[\\\"AC-6.1\\\",\\\"TC-6.1\\\"],\\\"recommendedFixScope\\\":\\\"fresh-fix-path\\\",\\\"blocking\\\":true},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"EV-002\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"major\\\",\\\"title\\\":\\\"Production --resume does not resolve an operator selection\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"AC-1.7 in epic.md:141 requires --resume to resolve the operator's selection. index.ts:210-226 only notifies the candidate list and returns the single candidate or null for multiple candidates; thread-resolution.test.ts:255-292 asserts fail-closed behavior for multiple candidates rather than a selected thread.\\\",\\\"affectedFiles\\\":[\\\"packages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts\\\",\\\"packages/pi-lhc/src/lifecycle/picker.ts\\\",\\\"packages/pi-lhc/test/lifecycle/thread-resolution.test.ts\\\"],\\\"requirementIds\\\":[\\\"AC-1.7\\\",\\\"TC-1.7\\\"],\\\"recommendedFixScope\\\":\\\"human-ruling\\\",\\\"blocking\\\":true},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"EV-003\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"major\\\",\\\"title\\\":\\\"Reload identity reintroduced a PI custom-entry mapping contrary to the tech design\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"tech-design.md:22 says there is no PI-session-to-thread mapping to persist, and tech-design.md:26 says appendEntry is moot for mapping because no session-file pointer is written. index.ts:134-158 defines pi-lhc-thread-state, index.ts:304-306 reads it from ctx.sessionManager.getEntries(), index.ts:468-469 appends the current threadRef, and index.ts:677-679 wires that append through pi.appendEntry.\\\",\\\"affectedFiles\\\":[\\\"docs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/tech-design.md\\\",\\\"packages/pi-lhc/src/index.ts\\\",\\\"packages/pi-lhc/test/lifecycle/thread-resolution.test.ts\\\"],\\\"requirementIds\\\":[\\\"AC-1.5\\\",\\\"TC-1.5\\\"],\\\"recommendedFixScope\\\":\\\"human-ruling\\\",\\\"blocking\\\":true}],\\\"nonBlockingFindings\\\":[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"NF-001\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"observation\\\",\\\"title\\\":\\\"Configured gates pass\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"Ran pnpm --filter pi-lhc verify and pnpm --filter pi-lhc verify-all; both completed successfully with 20 test files and 143 tests passing.\\\",\\\"affectedFiles\\\":[\\\"packages/pi-lhc/package.json\\\"],\\\"requirementIds\\\":[],\\\"recommendedFixScope\\\":\\\"same-session-implementor\\\",\\\"blocking\\\":false},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"NF-002\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"observation\\\",\\\"title\\\":\\\"Local PI and pi-ai type declarations are temporary boundaries\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"packages/pi-lhc/src/pi/types.ts declares the consumed PI API slice because the real package is not a workspace dependency; packages/pi-lhc/src/inference/pi-ai.ts provides local pi-ai typing while model-call dynamically imports @earendil-works/pi-ai at runtime.\\\",\\\"affectedFiles\\\":[\\\"packages/pi-lhc/src/pi/types.ts\\\",\\\"packages/pi-lhc/src/inference/pi-ai.ts\\\",\\\"packages/pi-lhc/src/inference/model-call.ts\\\"],\\\"requirementIds\\\":[\\\"AC-4.1\\\"],\\\"recommendedFixScope\\\":\\\"fresh-fix-path\\\",\\\"blocking\\\":false},{\\\"id\\\":\\\"NF-003\\\",\\\"severity\\\":\\\"observation\\\",\\\"title\\\":\\\"Derived-form reuse remains deferred but correctness is preserved\\\",\\\"evidence\\\":\\\"fork.ts seeds forked threads by replay and does not copy ready derived forms; AC-3.3 permits requeue when reuse safety cannot be proven.\\\",\\\"affectedFiles\\\":[\\\"packages/pi-lhc/src/lifecycle/fork.ts\\\",\\\"packages/pi-lhc/test/lifecycle/fork.test.ts\\\"],\\\"requirementIds\\\":[\\\"AC-3.3\\\",\\\"TC-3.3\\\"],\\\"recommendedFixScope\\\":\\\"fresh-fix-path\\\",\\\"blocking\\\":false}],\\\"unresolvedItems\\\":[\\\"Add real recorded PI corpora or formally narrow AC-6.1 before passing the epic.\\\",\\\"Human ruling or tech-design update needed for the PI custom-entry threadRef mapping introduced in index.ts.\\\",\\\"Human ruling or PI API change needed for --resume operator selection in production.\\\",\\\"If the custom-entry mapping is accepted, update the architecture docs and lifecycle tests so thread identity is consistently specified as registry threadId, including fork reload.\\\"],\\\"gateResult\\\":\\\"pass\\\"}\"}}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
