{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20260614215413b59feca83e00414e","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a comprehensive understanding of the implementation. Let me compile my epic review findings. I need to return a JSON object matching `EpicVerifierProviderPayload` that covers:\n\n1. Cross-story findings\n2. Architecture findings\n3. Epic coverage assessment\n4. Production path findings (fake adapters, shims, placeholders)\n5. Blocking findings\n6. Non-blocking findings\n7. Unresolved items\n8. Gate result\n\nLet me analyze what I've found:\n\n## Cross-Story Findings\n- The implementation follows the story breakdown consistently\n- Stories 0-4 are complete and verified\n- Stories 5-6 are implemented but the verification shows the \"deterministic/default\" assignments are being used (the warning output shows all 7 derivation kinds using deterministic/default)\n\n## Architecture Findings\n- The architecture aligns with the tech design\n- The plain-data-only rule is followed (SessionState holds only threadRef and flags)\n- The converter follows the two-stage design (map-message + turn-accumulator)\n- Turn derivation correctly ignores PI's per-step turn_end and emits at agent_end\n- Fork detection uses both hook-based and session-tree fallback\n- Idempotency uses the 4-tier system (entryId, toolCallId, responseId, fingerprint)\n\n## Production Path Audit\nKey finding: The pi-ai boundary\n- `createModelCall` in `inference/model-call.ts` dynamically imports `@earendil-works/pi-ai` at runtime (line 78-90)\n- If the package is not available, it returns a classified failure\n- The default assignments use `openai-codex/gpt-5.4` which is a real PI model (line 156)\n- Test fixtures use deterministic fakes (`fakeModelCallText`, `fakeModelCallFailure`, `fakeModelCallRouter`)\n- The closed-loop test (Story 5) proves the loop works with fakes but the production path is real\n\n\n- Local PI types are declared in `src/pi/types.ts` and `src/inference/pi-ai.ts` as a documented deviation\n\n## Coverage Assessment\nI see substantial test coverage across the epic's requirements. The test suite demonstrates comprehensive validation:\n- 142 tests successfully executed across 20 test files\n- Tests span multiple story levels, including lifecycle, capture, verification, fork, and inference scenarios\n- Verification through `pnpm --filter pi-lhc verify-all` confirms robust implementation\n\n## Risks and Unresolved Items\n- M0 corpora availability remains uncertain\n- Image/file reference handling lacks a final decision\n- Local PI type declarations represent a known deviation\n- The `initLhc` rename is currently unresolved\n\n## Blockers and Acceptance Status\nNo critical blocking findings emerged. The implementation appears sound, with only minor documentation and type-related deviations noted.\n\nThe production path demonstrates authenticity through its dynamic import mechanism and structured error handling. All stories have been accepted and committed, with the epic gate successfully passing verification. I'll format the comprehensive output as a JSON payload for clear presentation.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d8b1b04c-6358-4278-8fe4-779266fa40d8","uuid":"90d4e6e3-2044-4219-8f23-aff8fe7fce37"}
