{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_2026061421561170434080a4d54584","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a comprehensive view. Let me reconcile the findings:\n\n**Key Evidence:**\n\n1. **Corpus fixtures (EV-001)**: \n   - chatty.jsonl contains handcrafted AgentMessage data (simple role/content)\n   - replay.ts line 36-38 comment: \"synthetic PI source messages (the stand-in until M0 delivers recordings)\"\n   - Epic AC-6.1 requires \"recorded PI corpora\" with specific varieties\n   - Team impl-log shows Story 3 accepted with M0 inputs pending\n\n2. **Reload behavior (EV-003)**:\n   - thread-resolution.ts lines 141-147: for no-flag and --resume reload, uses cwd-most-recent\n   - Comment acknowledges \"cwd-most-recent reattach is an Epic-1 convenience\"\n   - Epic AC-1.5: \"reload reconstructs from durable state, the resolved thread id\"\n   - Test TC-1.5 (lines 336-373) proves reload works when no newer cwd thread exists\n   - Team impl-log shows SV-001 dispositioned as accepted-risk due to PI API constraints\n\n3. **--resume behavior (EV-002)**:\n   - index.ts lines 192-200: defaultSelectThread returns null for multiple candidates\n   - Test TC-1.7 (lines 255-293) shows it fails closed with multiple candidates\n   - Epic AC-1.7: \"resolves the operator's selection\"\n   - Team impl-log shows SV-002 dispositioned as accepted-risk due to PI v0.79.2 lacking interactive input API\n\n4. **LHC SDK exports (EF-001)**:\n   - Reviewer 1's EF-001 mentions FORM_KINDS, WORK_KIND_REGISTRY not in SDK_ONLY_EXPORTS\n   - Looking at sdk.ts lines 33-34 and 59-60, FORM_KINDS and WORK_KIND_REGISTRY ARE exported\n   - retirement.test.ts SDK_ONLY_EXPORTS list (lines 20-49) includes WORK_KIND_REGISTRY (line 27) but NOT FORM_KINDS\n   - Looking at sdk.ts line 33, FORM_KINDS is exported - so EF-001 may be partially correct about FORM_KINDS missing from the list\n\nLet me check if FORM_KINDS is actually in the SDK_ONLY_EXPORTS list... Looking at the list (lines 20-49), I see WORK_KIND_REGISTRY on line 27, but I don't see FORM_KINDS. However, FORM_KINDS is exported from sdk.ts line 33.\n\n**Reconciliation:**\n\nThe two reviewers fundamentally disagree on whether accepted-risk deviations should be blocking:\n\n- **Reviewer 1 (claude-code)**: outcome \"revise\" - treats the Story 1 deviations (SV-001, SV-002) as resolved via accepted-risk disposition, only surfaces EF-001 as non-blocking\n- **Reviewer 2 (codex)**: outcome \"block\" - treats EV-001, EV-002, EV-003 as blocking because the literal epic contract isn't satisfied\n\nFrom the code evidence:\n- EV-001 (corpora) is REAL - fixtures are synthetic, not \"recorded PI corpora\"\n- EV-002 (--resume) is REAL - returns null for multiple candidates, doesn't \"resolve operator's selection\"\n- EV-003 (reload) is REAL - uses cwd-most-recent, not exact thread reconstruction\n- EF-001 (exports) may have merit about FORM_KINDS\n\nThe team impl-log records that all Story 1/2 deviations were \"approved by impl-lead under Lee's standing authorization\" and documented as accepted-risk with upstream requests filed.\n\n**My role as reverifier**: I need to assess whether these findings remain supported by evidence and whether they should block the epic. The key question is whether \"accepted-risk\" deviations that don't meet the literal AC contract should block epic closeout.\n\nGiven that:\n1. The gates pass\n2. The deviations are documented with impl-lead approval\n3. The limitations are due to verified platform constraints (PI v0.79.2 API gaps)\n4. Upstream requests are filed\n\nI should NOT downgrade the blockers just because they're \"accepted-risk\" - that's exactly what Reviewer 2 is correctly identifying. The question is whether the epic can pass with these documented deviations.\n\nLet me now produce the canonical review.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_ma